window.QUIZ2_BANK.push(...[{"id":1,"targetId":1,"type":"tc","text":"Even after the disputed tax was withdrawn, public anger failed to {{1}}; protests continued with their original intensity.","choices":[["abate","proliferate","coalesce","oscillate","culminate"]],"answers":[["abate"]],"explanation":"Unchanged intensity shows that anger did not abate, or lessen. The other verbs suggest growth, combination, fluctuation, or culmination.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":2,"targetId":2,"type":"se","text":"Because every other sample behaved as predicted, the chemist treated the single result that violated the established pattern as {{1}}.","choices":[["aberrant","atypical","representative","orthodox","definitive","inevitable"]],"answers":[["aberrant","atypical"]],"explanation":"The exceptional result is aberrant or atypical. Normality, certainty, and necessity do not express departure from a pattern.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"atypical","meaning":"Departing from what is usual"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":3,"targetId":3,"type":"tc","text":"At first the unusually warm winter seemed an {{1}}, but its recurrence made the one-time-exception explanation increasingly {{2}}.","choices":[["aberration","inevitability","orthodoxy"],["discreditable","untenable","irrefutable"]],"answers":[["aberration"],["untenable"]],"explanation":"An isolated departure is an aberration. Repetition makes that explanation untenable, not irrefutable; discreditable concerns disgrace rather than explanatory adequacy.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"untenable","meaning":"Unable to be defended or sustained"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":4,"targetId":4,"type":"tc","text":"The accountant refused to {{1}} the fraud: providing the requested false invoices would make her an active participant.","choices":[["abet","expose","forestall","denounce","rectify"]],"answers":[["abet"]],"explanation":"False invoices would help, or abet, the fraud. The alternatives reveal, prevent, condemn, or correct it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":5,"targetId":5,"type":"tc","text":"The committee held the proposal in {{1}} during the inquiry, insisting that the pause was {{2}} and should not be mistaken for an irrevocable, {{3}} rejection.","choices":[["abeyance","ascendancy","disarray"],["provisional","permanent","belated"],["definitive","tentative","equivocal"]],"answers":[["abeyance"],["provisional"],["definitive"]],"explanation":"Suspension is abeyance. Provisional preserves the possibility of resuming; definitive rejection would close it. Permanent and tentative would reverse the intended distinctions.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"provisional","meaning":"Temporary and subject to later confirmation"},{"word":"definitive","meaning":"Final and decisive"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":6,"targetId":6,"type":"se","text":"Although willing to forgive ordinary mistakes, the editor came to {{1}} deliberate fabrication, which she regarded as an intolerable betrayal of readers.","choices":[["abhor","detest","condone","solicit","overlook","commend"]],"answers":[["abhor","detest"]],"explanation":"Intolerable betrayal supports abhor and detest. The alternatives permit, request, ignore, or praise fabrication.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"detest","meaning":"Feel intense hatred or dislike"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":7,"targetId":7,"type":"tc","text":"The referee would {{1}} criticism of her decisions, but she stopped the match when criticism turned into threats.","choices":[["abide","provoke","extol","manufacture","prohibit"]],"answers":[["abide"]],"explanation":"The contrast makes criticism tolerable and threats unacceptable. Abide means tolerate; prohibit erases the distinction.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":8,"targetId":8,"type":"tc","text":"The expedition ended in {{1}} failure, yet the leader's account was remarkably {{2}}, dwelling on imaginary triumphs rather than acknowledging the disaster.","choices":[["abject","qualified","incipient"],["triumphalist","penitent","dispassionate"]],"answers":[["abject"],["triumphalist"]],"explanation":"Disaster indicates abject failure. Imaginary triumphs make the account triumphalist, not repentant or impartial.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"triumphalist","meaning":"Excessively celebrating victory or success"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":9,"targetId":9,"type":"tc","text":"To regain his position, the dissident was ordered to {{1}} the very doctrine he had spent decades publicly defending.","choices":[["abjure","disseminate","elucidate","corroborate","exemplify"]],"answers":[["abjure"]],"explanation":"The required reversal is to solemnly reject, or abjure, the doctrine. The other actions preserve or advance it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":10,"targetId":10,"type":"tc","text":"Explanations for the collapse {{1}}, but evidence supporting any one of them remains {{2}}; plentiful theories should not be confused with certainty.","choices":[["abound","converge","dwindle"],["exiguous","decisive","unassailable"]],"answers":[["abound"],["exiguous"]],"explanation":"Many explanations abound, while little evidence is exiguous. Decisive evidence would undermine the warning about uncertainty.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exiguous","meaning":"Very scanty in amount"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":11,"targetId":11,"type":"tc","text":"Publishing an annual review was insufficient to keep readers {{1}} of discoveries in a field where major results appeared every week.","choices":[["abreast","bereft","heedless","independent","contemptuous"]],"answers":[["abreast"]],"explanation":"Frequent discoveries require frequent updates to stay abreast, or up to date. None of the alternatives expresses currency of knowledge.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":12,"targetId":12,"type":"se","text":"The treasurer planned to {{1}} with the funds before the audit, leaving town secretly rather than facing the investigators.","choices":[["abscond","decamp","negotiate","testify","linger","capitulate"]],"answers":[["abscond","decamp"]],"explanation":"Secret departure supports abscond and decamp. The other actions involve staying or engaging with the situation rather than fleeing.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"decamp","meaning":"Leave suddenly or secretly to escape"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":13,"targetId":13,"type":"tc","text":"Despite being eligible to vote, members with a financial stake chose to {{1}}; they considered their participation {{2}} with the panel's promise of impartiality.","choices":[["abstain","prevail","intercede"],["incompatible","consonant","commensurate"]],"answers":[["abstain"],["incompatible"]],"explanation":"They abstain, voluntarily refraining, because their financial interests are incompatible with impartiality. Agreement and proportion do not express this conflict.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"incompatible","meaning":"Unable to coexist harmoniously"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":14,"targetId":14,"type":"tc","text":"The seminar's discussion of justice remained {{1}} until a participant asked how the principle would apply to an actual eviction.","choices":[["abstract","anecdotal","pragmatic","empirical","parochial"]],"answers":[["abstract"]],"explanation":"The transition from principles to a real case contrasts abstract thought with concrete application; anecdotal and empirical already involve observations.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":15,"targetId":15,"type":"tc","text":"The paper's argument was so {{1}} that even specialists struggled to follow it; the editor requested a more {{2}} exposition because needless complexity would {{3}} its central insight.","choices":[["abstruse","pellucid","banal"],["perspicuous","convoluted","grandiloquent"],["occlude","ratify","illuminate"]],"answers":[["abstruse"],["perspicuous"],["occlude"]],"explanation":"Difficulty following the argument makes it abstruse. Perspicuous clarity is needed because complexity may occlude the insight, not illuminate or validate it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"perspicuous","meaning":"Clearly expressed and readily understood"},{"word":"occlude","meaning":"Block or obscure"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":16,"targetId":16,"type":"se","text":"Rather than disguise the building's irregular shape, the lighting was designed to {{1}} it, drawing visitors' attention to the unusual outline.","choices":[["accentuate","emphasize","obliterate","regularize","camouflage","minimize"]],"answers":[["accentuate","emphasize"]],"explanation":"Drawing attention means accentuating or emphasizing. The alternatives remove, regularize, conceal, or diminish prominence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"emphasize","meaning":"Give particular prominence to"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":17,"targetId":17,"type":"tc","text":"The revised handbook is {{1}} to beginners because its examples assume no prior knowledge of the subject.","choices":[["accessible","impenetrable","esoteric","derivative","inconsequential"]],"answers":[["accessible"]],"explanation":"No prerequisite knowledge makes it accessible. The alternatives either oppose understandability or describe unrelated qualities.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":18,"targetId":18,"type":"tc","text":"The director enjoyed international {{1}}, but at home the same films met with {{2}}, their unconventional endings provoking hostile reviews.","choices":[["acclaim","ostracism","obscurity"],["obloquy","adulation","indifference"]],"answers":[["acclaim"],["obloquy"]],"explanation":"International praise is acclaim. Hostile reviews provide contrasting obloquy, rather than further praise or indifference.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"obloquy","meaning":"Strong public condemnation or abusive criticism"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":19,"targetId":19,"type":"tc","text":"Although the prize was the most prestigious {{1}} of her career, she valued the students' quiet gratitude more.","choices":[["accolade","impediment","indictment","anomaly","contingency"]],"answers":[["accolade"]],"explanation":"A prestigious prize is an accolade, or honor. Obstacles, accusations, exceptions, and possibilities do not describe its honorific status.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":20,"targetId":20,"type":"tc","text":"His response was {{1}}: instead of disputing the calculation calmly, he called its author incompetent, turning a technical disagreement into a {{2}} exchange.","choices":[["acerbic","placatory","noncommittal"],["vituperative","dispassionate","perfunctory"]],"answers":[["acerbic"],["vituperative"]],"explanation":"The personal insult makes the response acerbic and the exchange vituperative. Placatory and dispassionate contradict the hostility; perfunctory describes effort rather than abuse.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"vituperative","meaning":"Bitterly abusive or denunciatory"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":21,"targetId":21,"type":"tc","text":"Once an unquestioning {{1}} of the philosopher, Mira eventually rejected the master's assumptions and founded a competing school.","choices":[["acolyte","antagonist","arbiter","benefactor","precursor"]],"answers":[["acolyte"]],"explanation":"Unquestioning devotion identifies an acolyte, or follower. An antagonist would already oppose the philosopher.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":22,"targetId":22,"type":"se","text":"Rather than {{1}} in the new restrictions, the researchers organized a formal challenge, refusing to accept rules they considered unjust.","choices":[["acquiesce","concur","remonstrate","intervene","deliberate","persevere"]],"answers":[["acquiesce","concur"]],"explanation":"Acquiesce and concur express the acceptance being rejected. The alternatives do not mean accepting the restrictions.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"concur","meaning":"Agree or assent"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":23,"targetId":23,"type":"tc","text":"The collector's {{1}} impulses outstripped his income; even treasured possessions became {{2}} once he noticed something else to purchase.","choices":[["acquisitive","ascetic","altruistic"],["dispensable","inviolable","irreplaceable"]],"answers":[["acquisitive"],["dispensable"]],"explanation":"Relentless purchasing is acquisitive. Old possessions become dispensable as new desires replace them, not inviolable or irreplaceable.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"dispensable","meaning":"Able to be given up or done without"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":24,"targetId":24,"type":"tc","text":"The negotiations became so {{1}} that the mediator banned personal accusations before any further discussion of terms.","choices":[["acrimonious","convivial","perfunctory","dispassionate","fruitful"]],"answers":[["acrimonious"]],"explanation":"Personal accusations reveal bitter hostility, or acrimony. Friendly or detached negotiations would not require this intervention.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":25,"targetId":25,"type":"tc","text":"Years of public {{1}} had made cooperation seem impossible, yet the project revealed a surprising {{2}}: each rival supplied what the other lacked, making their skills {{3}} rather than redundant.","choices":[["acrimony","comity","deference"],["synergy","schism","stalemate"],["complementary","interchangeable","antithetical"]],"answers":[["acrimony"],["synergy"],["complementary"]],"explanation":"Bitter relations are acrimony. Combining different strengths creates synergy and makes skills complementary; redundancy would imply interchangeability.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"synergy","meaning":"An enhanced combined effect of cooperation"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":26,"targetId":26,"type":"se","text":"The investor's {{1}} was evident in her ability to recognize an unsustainable business model long before celebrated analysts noticed its flaws.","choices":[["acumen","discernment","credulity","temerity","indolence","munificence"]],"answers":[["acumen","discernment"]],"explanation":"Early accurate judgment supports acumen and discernment. Gullibility, rashness, laziness, and generosity concern different qualities.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"discernment","meaning":"Ability to judge and distinguish accurately"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":27,"targetId":27,"type":"tc","text":"Even after the board offered twice the original fee, the architect remained {{1}} that the unsafe design must be changed.","choices":[["adamant","ambivalent","indifferent","pliant","contrite"]],"answers":[["adamant"]],"explanation":"The increased fee cannot alter her firm position: she is adamant. Pliancy or ambivalence would weaken that insistence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":28,"targetId":28,"type":"tc","text":"Though {{1}} at finding faults in other people's proposals, the consultant proved {{2}} when asked to develop a workable alternative herself.","choices":[["adept","ineffectual","reticent"],["inept","resourceful","proficient"]],"answers":[["adept"],["inept"]],"explanation":"The contrast opposes adept criticism to inept construction. Resourceful and proficient would remove the contrast.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"inept","meaning":"Lacking skill or competence"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":29,"targetId":29,"type":"tc","text":"The supervisor chose to {{1}} the intern privately for the breach rather than allow the mistake to pass without comment.","choices":[["admonish","exonerate","emulate","commend","indulge"]],"answers":[["admonish"]],"explanation":"Addressing the breach requires a warning or rebuke, admonishment. Praise, clearance, imitation, and indulgence do not express that response.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":30,"targetId":30,"type":"tc","text":"The historian's final chapter is {{1}}, warning that complacency invites disaster; its tone is therefore {{2}}, despite the preceding chapters' optimism.","choices":[["admonitory","laudatory","nostalgic"],["cautionary","jubilant","flippant"]],"answers":[["admonitory"],["cautionary"]],"explanation":"The warning makes both admonitory and cautionary appropriate. Celebration, praise, and flippancy would contradict its purpose.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"cautionary","meaning":"Serving as a warning about possible danger"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":31,"targetId":31,"type":"tc","text":"An {{1}} negotiator, Sana found wording that preserved both parties' core demands without disguising their genuine differences.","choices":[["adroit","inept","doctrinaire","heedless","diffident"]],"answers":[["adroit"]],"explanation":"Resourceful reconciliation demonstrates adroitness. Incompetence, rigidity, and carelessness would obstruct this achievement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":32,"targetId":32,"type":"se","text":"Investigators found that the supplier had tried to {{1}} the expensive oil with a cheap substitute, reducing its purity while retaining the premium price.","choices":[["adulterate","debase","refine","authenticate","distill","segregate"]],"answers":[["adulterate","debase"]],"explanation":"An inferior addition adulterates or debases the oil. Refining and distilling do not mean reducing purity through inferior additions.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"debase","meaning":"Lower the quality or value of"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":33,"targetId":33,"type":"tc","text":"The reviewers reported {{1}} effects, but the company presented these harms as {{2}}, dismissing them as too slight to influence purchasing decisions.","choices":[["adverse","salutary","propitious"],["negligible","catastrophic","pervasive"]],"answers":[["adverse"],["negligible"]],"explanation":"Harms are adverse effects. Calling them too slight is calling them negligible; catastrophic and pervasive would acknowledge importance.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":34,"targetId":34,"type":"tc","text":"Though personally indifferent to the policy, the minister agreed to {{1}} it publicly, defending it repeatedly in parliamentary debates.","choices":[["advocate","repudiate","obstruct","dissemble","abrogate"]],"answers":[["advocate"]],"explanation":"Public defense constitutes advocacy. Repudiation, obstruction, and abrogation oppose a policy; dissembling does not identify support.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":35,"targetId":35,"type":"tc","text":"An {{1}} concerned chiefly with color and proportion, the patron found the engineer's practical objections {{2}}; the engineer dismissed such artistic priorities as {{3}} to structural safety.","choices":[["aesthete","iconoclast","empiricist"],["prosaic","rapturous","ornamental"],["extraneous","indispensable","integral"]],"answers":[["aesthete"],["prosaic"],["extraneous"]],"explanation":"Devotion to beauty identifies an aesthete. Practical objections seem prosaic to that patron; artistic priorities seem extraneous rather than essential to the engineer's safety assessment.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":36,"targetId":36,"type":"se","text":"The curator's objection was {{1}} rather than financial: she disliked the installation's composition even though its cost was modest.","choices":[["aesthetic","artistic","pecuniary","logistical","statutory","chronological"]],"answers":[["aesthetic","artistic"]],"explanation":"Composition prompts aesthetic or artistic judgment. The alternatives concern money, arrangements, law, and time.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"artistic","meaning":"Relating to art or its appreciation"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":37,"targetId":37,"type":"tc","text":"Her elaborate accent was an {{1}}: recordings revealed that she spoke quite differently when no wealthy patrons were listening.","choices":[["affectation","affliction","innovation","inhibition","epiphany"]],"answers":[["affectation"]],"explanation":"An accent adopted for patrons is an artificial manner meant to impress: an affectation. The alternatives do not capture this performance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":38,"targetId":38,"type":"tc","text":"Their shared interest in obscure maps created an immediate {{1}}, but disputes over ownership soon made their partnership {{2}}, prone to splitting apart.","choices":[["affinity","aversion","disparity"],["fissiparous","unassailable","fraternal"]],"answers":[["affinity"],["fissiparous"]],"explanation":"Shared interests generate affinity. Ownership disputes make the partnership fissiparous; unassailable and fraternal suggest strength or closeness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"fissiparous","meaning":"Inclined to divide or break apart"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":39,"targetId":39,"type":"tc","text":"Excluding the country's delegates from a ceremony honoring their own discovery was interpreted as an intentional {{1}}.","choices":[["affront","concession","reprieve","accolade","enticement"]],"answers":[["affront"]],"explanation":"The exclusion is an insult, or affront. An accolade or concession would convey respect or accommodation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":40,"targetId":40,"type":"tc","text":"The reform ostensibly decentralized authority, but obscure provisions allowed the president to {{1}} his office and made provincial autonomy largely {{2}}.","choices":[["aggrandize","curtail","abdicate"],["illusory","substantive","inviolable"]],"answers":[["aggrandize"],["illusory"]],"explanation":"The hidden centralization aggrandizes presidential power. Provincial autonomy exists only in appearance, making it illusory rather than substantive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":41,"targetId":41,"type":"tc","text":"The organizers continued to {{1}} for safer working conditions even after management warned them to stop campaigning.","choices":[["agitate","atone","capitulate","convalesce","prevaricate"]],"answers":[["agitate"]],"explanation":"Campaigning persistently for reform is to agitate. The alternatives concern repentance, surrender, recovery, and evasion.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":42,"targetId":42,"type":"se","text":"The usually reluctant volunteers accepted this assignment with {{1}}, arriving early and eagerly requesting additional tasks.","choices":[["alacrity","eagerness","trepidation","reluctance","indifference","solemnity"]],"answers":[["alacrity","eagerness"]],"explanation":"Readiness for extra work supports alacrity and eagerness. Anxiety, reluctance, indifference, and solemnity do not convey the same enthusiasm.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"eagerness","meaning":"Enthusiastic readiness to act"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":43,"targetId":43,"type":"tc","text":"The chair's contemptuous jokes began to {{1}} even her allies, turning formerly {{2}} colleagues into critics.","choices":[["alienate","mollify","recruit"],["supportive","antagonistic","disaffected"]],"answers":[["alienate"],["supportive"]],"explanation":"Contempt estranges, or alienates, allies. Those former allies were supportive; already antagonistic colleagues would not undergo the stated change.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"supportive","meaning":"Providing help or encouragement"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":44,"targetId":44,"type":"tc","text":"The grant could {{1}} the village's shortage of drinking water, but officials conceded that a permanent solution required a new supply network.","choices":[["alleviate","exacerbate","conceal","precipitate","diagnose"]],"answers":[["alleviate"]],"explanation":"A helpful but incomplete measure alleviates the shortage. Diagnosis or concealment would not itself improve water access.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":45,"targetId":45,"type":"tc","text":"The poem is {{1}}, gesturing toward an ancient legend without naming it; without that background, readers may find its imagery {{2}} rather than {{3}}.","choices":[["allusive","expository","documentary"],["cryptic","transparent","mundane"],["perspicuous","opaque","inscrutable"]],"answers":[["allusive"],["cryptic"],["perspicuous"]],"explanation":"Indirect reference makes the poem allusive. Missing context makes imagery cryptic rather than perspicuous; opaque and inscrutable would not provide the needed contrast.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"perspicuous","meaning":"Clearly expressed and readily understood"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":46,"targetId":46,"type":"se","text":"Even at celebrations arranged in his honor, the scholar remained {{1}}, keeping apart from guests and showing little interest in their conversation.","choices":[["aloof","distant","gregarious","effusive","solicitous","animated"]],"answers":[["aloof","distant"]],"explanation":"Aloof and distant describe his detachment. Sociability, enthusiasm, and concern contradict keeping apart without interest.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"distant","meaning":"Socially or emotionally detached"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":47,"targetId":47,"type":"tc","text":"Her donation was unusually {{1}}: it remained anonymous, carried no tax benefit, and went to strangers she would never meet.","choices":[["altruistic","mercenary","ostentatious","coercive","parsimonious"]],"answers":[["altruistic"]],"explanation":"The absence of personal reward supports altruism. Mercenary and ostentatious motives are explicitly undermined by the details.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":48,"targetId":48,"type":"tc","text":"The city's cuisine is an {{1}} of immigrant traditions, though tourism campaigns falsely portrayed it as {{2}}, untouched by outside influence.","choices":[["amalgam","antithesis","abeyance"],["autochthonous","hybrid","cosmopolitan"]],"answers":[["amalgam"],["autochthonous"]],"explanation":"A mixture is an amalgam. The false claim of purely local origin describes it as autochthonous; hybrid and cosmopolitan acknowledge outside influences.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"autochthonous","meaning":"Indigenous or originating locally"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":49,"targetId":49,"type":"tc","text":"Rather than retain three overlapping archives, the university decided to {{1}} them into a single searchable collection.","choices":[["amalgamate","disperse","sequester","repudiate","abridge"]],"answers":[["amalgamate"]],"explanation":"Combining archives means amalgamating them. Abridging shortens contents without necessarily joining collections.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":50,"targetId":50,"type":"tc","text":"The treaty's language was deliberately {{1}}, allowing each side to claim victory; this semantic {{2}} postponed rather than resolved their disagreement.","choices":[["ambiguous","unequivocal","univocal"],["indeterminacy","precision","consensus"]],"answers":[["ambiguous"],["indeterminacy"]],"explanation":"Rival interpretations make the treaty ambiguous. Semantic indeterminacy permits conflicting claims; precision and consensus would not leave the dispute unresolved.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"indeterminacy","meaning":"Lack of a fixed or definite meaning or outcome"}],"section":1,"initial":"a"},{"id":51,"targetId":51,"type":"tc","text":"The critic was {{1}} about the restoration: she welcomed the building's renewed usefulness but regretted the loss of its weathered character.","choices":[["ambivalent","indifferent","unanimous","unreserved","hostile"]],"answers":[["ambivalent"]],"explanation":"Welcoming one aspect while regretting another demonstrates conflicting feelings, or ambivalence. Indifference would mean not caring, and unreserved approval would exclude regret.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":52,"targetId":52,"type":"se","text":"The policy was intended to {{1}} conditions in overcrowded schools, making them better rather than simply preventing further deterioration.","choices":[["ameliorate","improve","entrench","aggravate","document","perpetuate"]],"answers":[["ameliorate","improve"]],"explanation":"Making conditions better requires ameliorate and improve. Documenting records conditions without improving them; entrenching and perpetuating preserve them.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"improve","meaning":"Make better"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":53,"targetId":53,"type":"tc","text":"The director was surprisingly {{1}} to the proposed revisions; instead of defending her draft as sacrosanct, she treated the critics' comments as {{2}}.","choices":[["amenable","impervious","antipathetic"],["constructive","libelous","impertinent"]],"answers":[["amenable"],["constructive"]],"explanation":"Willingness to accept revisions makes her amenable. She sees criticism as constructive help, rather than defamatory or disrespectful interference.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"constructive","meaning":"Useful in producing improvement"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":54,"targetId":54,"type":"tc","text":"Since the original statute did not address online transactions, legislators agreed to {{1}} it by adding provisions for digital commerce.","choices":[["amend","repeal","disregard","subvert","canonize"]],"answers":[["amend"]],"explanation":"Adding provisions revises, or amends, the statute. Repeal would remove it, and canonization would elevate rather than revise it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":55,"targetId":55,"type":"tc","text":"Although the partners' financial interests had diverged, their separation remained {{1}}; each made {{2}} concessions, and neither used the occasion to {{3}} old grievances.","choices":[["amicable","acrimonious","coercive"],["reciprocal","unilateral","nominal"],["vent","assuage","forget"]],"answers":[["amicable"],["reciprocal"],["vent"]],"explanation":"A peaceful separation is amicable. Concessions made by each side are reciprocal, and avoiding a hostile airing of grievances means not venting them. Unilateral would describe only one side yielding.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"reciprocal","meaning":"Given or done mutually"},{"word":"vent","meaning":"Express or release an emotion forcefully"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":56,"targetId":56,"type":"se","text":"The movement remained {{1}}, with no agreed boundaries or organizing structure, so observers could not identify a stable form beneath its changing demands.","choices":[["amorphous","shapeless","regimented","hierarchical","monolithic","ossified"]],"answers":[["amorphous","shapeless"]],"explanation":"The absence of stable form supports amorphous and shapeless. The other choices imply fixed structure, organization, or rigidity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"shapeless","meaning":"Lacking definite form or structure"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":57,"targetId":57,"type":"tc","text":"The film's reconstruction was painstaking, making the electric clock in its medieval courtroom especially {{1}}.","choices":[["anachronistic","anachoretic","verisimilar","propitious","indigenous"]],"answers":[["anachronistic"]],"explanation":"An electric clock belongs to a later historical period, making it anachronistic. Verisimilar suggests realism, propitious suggests favorable prospects, indigenous concerns local origin, and anachoretic concerns solitary religious retreat.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":58,"targetId":58,"type":"tc","text":"The two institutions are {{1}} in function, but not identical in origin; treating the resemblance as proof of a shared ancestry would be {{2}}.","choices":[["analogous","antithetical","irrelevant"],["unwarranted","conclusive","obligatory"]],"answers":[["analogous"],["unwarranted"]],"explanation":"Comparable functions are analogous. Similarity alone does not prove ancestry, making the inference unwarranted rather than conclusive or obligatory.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unwarranted","meaning":"Not justified by sufficient reasons or evidence"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":59,"targetId":59,"type":"tc","text":"The exchange of insults deepened their {{1}}, transforming an ordinary scholarly disagreement into a lasting personal feud.","choices":[["animosity","affinity","comity","credulity","ambivalence"]],"answers":[["animosity"]],"explanation":"Insults and a feud indicate strong hostility, or animosity. Affinity and comity imply closeness; ambivalence would require mixed feelings.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":60,"targetId":60,"type":"tc","text":"The review's scholarly appearance concealed a personal {{1}}: the critic selected evidence so {{2}} that every omission favored his longstanding adversary's humiliation.","choices":[["animus","alacrity","affinity"],["tendentiously","impartially","haphazardly"]],"answers":[["animus"],["tendentiously"]],"explanation":"Personal hostility is animus. Systematic selection to humiliate an adversary is tendentious, not impartial or random.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"tendentiously","meaning":"In a biased manner advancing a particular position"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":61,"targetId":61,"type":"tc","text":"Although several candidates remained, the newspaper effectively chose to {{1}} the youngest as the party's future leader, treating her accession as settled.","choices":[["anoint","disqualify","interrogate","repudiate","ostracize"]],"answers":[["anoint"]],"explanation":"Treating a candidate as already chosen is figuratively anointing her. The alternatives do not endorse her selection.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":62,"targetId":62,"type":"se","text":"The village's prosperity was {{1}} within the impoverished region, departing so sharply from neighboring conditions that researchers investigated it separately.","choices":[["anomalous","atypical","ubiquitous","representative","customary","inevitable"]],"answers":[["anomalous","atypical"]],"explanation":"The marked departure from surrounding conditions makes prosperity anomalous or atypical. Representative and customary would erase its exceptional status.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"atypical","meaning":"Departing from what is usual"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":63,"targetId":63,"type":"tc","text":"What appeared to be a statistical {{1}} became the basis of a new theory once repeated experiments showed that the result was {{2}}.","choices":[["anomaly","truism","axiom"],["reproducible","illusory","fortuitous"]],"answers":[["anomaly"],["reproducible"]],"explanation":"An unexpected result is an anomaly. Repeated confirmation makes it reproducible, not illusory or merely accidental, giving it theoretical importance.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"reproducible","meaning":"Able to be obtained again under the same conditions"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":64,"targetId":64,"type":"tc","text":"Rather than win the board's support, the consultant managed to {{1}} its members by describing all their earlier work as worthless.","choices":[["antagonize","reassure","enchant","absolve","conciliate"]],"answers":[["antagonize"]],"explanation":"Calling their work worthless provokes hostility, so antagonize fits. The other verbs suggest reassurance, attraction, forgiveness, or reconciliation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":65,"targetId":65,"type":"tc","text":"The newly found letters {{1}} the supposed first formulation of the theory by a decade, so the familiar chronology requires {{2}}; their authenticity, however, must be established before that revision becomes {{3}}.","choices":[["antedate","postdate","coincide"],["reconsideration","ratification","neglect"],["defensible","superfluous","anachronistic"]],"answers":[["antedate"],["reconsideration"],["defensible"]],"explanation":"Earlier letters antedate the formulation. They call for reconsideration of chronology, but authentication is needed to make the revised account defensible rather than premature.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"reconsideration","meaning":"A fresh examination of a previous judgment"},{"word":"defensible","meaning":"Capable of being justified with reasons or evidence"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":66,"targetId":66,"type":"se","text":"Her {{1}} toward publicity was so strong that she declined even interviews designed to celebrate her achievements.","choices":[["antipathy","aversion","penchant","affinity","indifference","ambivalence"]],"answers":[["antipathy","aversion"]],"explanation":"Declining flattering interviews shows strong dislike, supporting antipathy and aversion. Indifference or mixed feelings is weaker and not the stated attitude.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":67,"targetId":67,"type":"tc","text":"The spare, unadorned room was the {{1}} of the lavish reception hall next door.","choices":[["antithesis","epitome","precursor","corollary","amalgam"]],"answers":[["antithesis"]],"explanation":"Spare and lavish directly oppose one another, making one the antithesis of the other. An epitome would exemplify the same qualities.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":68,"targetId":68,"type":"tc","text":"The constitutional lawyer argued that arbitrary detention was {{1}} to the charter's central commitments, so a decree authorizing it could not be considered merely {{2}}.","choices":[["antithetical","consonant","incidental"],["administrative","subversive","unconstitutional"]],"answers":[["antithetical"],["administrative"]],"explanation":"Conflict with central principles makes detention antithetical to them. Such a decree exceeds routine administrative business; calling it merely unconstitutional would not express the distinction between fundamental change and implementation.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"administrative","meaning":"Concerned with routine management or implementation"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":69,"targetId":69,"type":"tc","text":"Residents once indifferent to zoning became far less {{1}} when they learned that the proposed road would destroy the local park.","choices":[["apathetic","agitated","partisan","adamant","assiduous"]],"answers":[["apathetic"]],"explanation":"The news increases involvement, so residents become less apathetic. Less agitated or assiduous would suggest declining engagement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":70,"targetId":70,"type":"tc","text":"The campaign failed less because of active opposition than because of public {{1}}; its organizers could counter criticism, but struggled to overcome such {{2}}.","choices":[["apathy","acrimony","zeal"],["unresponsiveness","intransigence","vehemence"]],"answers":[["apathy"],["unresponsiveness"]],"explanation":"Lack of interest is apathy and unresponsiveness. Intransigence and vehemence imply active positions, unlike the problem described.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unresponsiveness","meaning":"Failure to react or show interest"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":71,"targetId":71,"type":"tc","text":"The celebrated anecdote is probably {{1}}: it first appeared long after the scientist's death, and no contemporary source supports it.","choices":[["apocryphal","axiomatic","verifiable","canonical","incidental"]],"answers":[["apocryphal"]],"explanation":"Late appearance without contemporary support casts doubt on authenticity, making apocryphal appropriate. Being canonical would concern acceptance, not truth.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":72,"targetId":72,"type":"se","text":"The award marked the {{1}} of her career, a high point of influence and achievement that none of her later accomplishments surpassed.","choices":[["apogee","zenith","nadir","inception","interregnum","denouement"]],"answers":[["apogee","zenith"]],"explanation":"An unsurpassed high point is both an apogee and a zenith. Nadir is a low point; the other terms locate stages rather than maximum achievement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":73,"targetId":73,"type":"tc","text":"Though presented as an independent historian, the author served as an {{1}} for the regime, offering {{2}} explanations for even its most indefensible decisions.","choices":[["apologist","iconoclast","antagonist"],["exculpatory","incriminating","derogatory"]],"answers":[["apologist"],["exculpatory"]],"explanation":"Defending the regime makes the author its apologist. Excusing its decisions requires exculpatory explanations, not ones that incriminate or disparage it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exculpatory","meaning":"Tending to clear someone of blame"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":74,"targetId":74,"type":"tc","text":"The concession was meant to {{1}} the strikers by meeting their principal demand, not to intimidate them into abandoning it.","choices":[["appease","provoke","discredit","coerce","polarize"]],"answers":[["appease"]],"explanation":"Meeting a demand to calm opposition is appeasement. Coercion explicitly contrasts with that method, and the other verbs intensify or attack opposition.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":75,"targetId":75,"type":"tc","text":"Initial {{1}} about the untested procedure yielded to {{2}} after repeated safe trials; nevertheless, the researchers warned that confidence should not become {{3}}.","choices":[["apprehension","euphoria","equanimity"],["assurance","foreboding","despair"],["complacency","vigilance","skepticism"]],"answers":[["apprehension"],["assurance"],["complacency"]],"explanation":"An untested procedure prompts apprehension. Safe trials bring assurance, but overlooking remaining risks would be complacency; vigilance and skepticism preserve caution.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"assurance","meaning":"Confidence or freedom from doubt"},{"word":"complacency","meaning":"Self-satisfaction that overlooks possible danger"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":76,"targetId":76,"type":"se","text":"The mayor sought public {{1}} for the redevelopment plan, hoping that an unmistakable expression of support would persuade hesitant lenders.","choices":[["approbation","approval","opprobrium","indifference","forbearance","clemency"]],"answers":[["approbation","approval"]],"explanation":"An expression of support is approbation or approval. Forbearance and clemency concern tolerance or mercy, not endorsement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"approval","meaning":"Favorable acceptance or agreement"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":77,"targetId":77,"type":"tc","text":"The invaders attempted to {{1}} the community's ceremonial objects, placing them in private collections without their owners' consent.","choices":[["appropriate","repatriate","consecrate","renounce","authenticate"]],"answers":[["appropriate"]],"explanation":"Taking objects for one's own use is the verbal sense of appropriate. Repatriation would return them, and authentication only establishes genuineness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":78,"targetId":78,"type":"tc","text":"The quotation was {{1}} to the debate, but the speaker's long account of its publication history was {{2}}, contributing nothing to the disputed issue.","choices":[["apropos","extraneous","antithetical"],["tangential","indispensable","decisive"]],"answers":[["apropos"],["tangential"]],"explanation":"A relevant quotation is apropos. Publication history that does not advance the dispute is tangential rather than indispensable or decisive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":79,"targetId":79,"type":"tc","text":"The comparison to an orchestra was particularly {{1}}: the project succeeded only when specialists coordinated their different contributions.","choices":[["apt","spurious","incongruous","anachronistic","facile"]],"answers":[["apt"]],"explanation":"Coordination among different specialists makes the orchestra comparison suitable, or apt. Spurious and incongruous would deny that fit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":80,"targetId":80,"type":"tc","text":"The allocation seemed {{1}} because identical applications received opposite decisions; without stated criteria, officials could not make the process appear {{2}}.","choices":[["arbitrary","methodical","inevitable"],["principled","capricious","opaque"]],"answers":[["arbitrary"],["principled"]],"explanation":"Unexplained inconsistent decisions look arbitrary. Clear criteria would make allocation principled; capricious and opaque restate rather than repair the problem.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"principled","meaning":"Guided by consistent standards or moral rules"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":81,"targetId":81,"type":"tc","text":"The code was so {{1}} that only a handful of initiates could read it, although to them its symbols were perfectly clear.","choices":[["arcane","universal","prosaic","transparent","obsolete"]],"answers":[["arcane"]],"explanation":"Restricted specialist knowledge makes the code arcane. Obsolete would mean out of use, which is not implied by its continuing use among initiates.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":82,"targetId":82,"type":"se","text":"The guide's terminology was {{1}}, employing expressions that had disappeared from ordinary scientific writing generations earlier.","choices":[["archaic","antiquated","innovative","neoteric","universal","idiomatic"]],"answers":[["archaic","antiquated"]],"explanation":"Long-abandoned terminology is archaic or antiquated. Innovative and neoteric suggest novelty; idiomatic and universal do not imply age.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"antiquated","meaning":"Outdated or belonging to an earlier era"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":83,"targetId":83,"type":"tc","text":"Later adventure novels repeatedly borrowed from this story, treating its resourceful outsider as an {{1}} rather than a {{2}} invention.","choices":[["archetype","aberration","afterthought"],["sui generis","prototypical","representative"]],"answers":[["archetype"],["sui generis"]],"explanation":"Repeated borrowing makes the character a model, or archetype. That contrasts with treating it as uniquely sui generis rather than representative or prototypical.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"sui generis","meaning":"Unique; forming a class of its own"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":84,"targetId":84,"type":"tc","text":"An {{1}} defender of public libraries, the historian spent weekends campaigning for them even when the cause offered no professional advantage.","choices":[["ardent","apathetic","perfunctory","mercenary","reluctant"]],"answers":[["ardent"]],"explanation":"Sustained voluntary campaigning reveals intense enthusiasm, or ardor. A mercenary motive is contradicted by the lack of professional reward.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":85,"targetId":85,"type":"tc","text":"The archive's index made locating an item easy, but restoring it remained {{1}}; the process required {{2}} attention to fragile details and offered no {{3}} shortcut.","choices":[["arduous","effortless","perfunctory"],["meticulous","cursory","desultory"],["expeditious","onerous","laborious"]],"answers":[["arduous"],["meticulous"],["expeditious"]],"explanation":"Restoration is difficult and meticulous even when location is easy. The absence of an expeditious shortcut underscores that difficulty; onerous and laborious would not describe a useful shortcut.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"expeditious","meaning":"Accomplished quickly and efficiently"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":86,"targetId":86,"type":"se","text":"The photograph was so {{1}} that visitors stopped midway through conversations to study it, unable to dismiss its startling composition.","choices":[["arresting","striking","banal","insipid","unobtrusive","conventional"]],"answers":[["arresting","striking"]],"explanation":"Attention captured by startling composition makes the image arresting and striking. The other choices suggest dullness or lack of prominence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"striking","meaning":"Attracting strong attention through unusual qualities"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":87,"targetId":87,"type":"tc","text":"The theft depended on an {{1}} substitution: a convincing replica occupied the case while the original was carried away unnoticed.","choices":[["artful","artless","inept","transparent","fortuitous"]],"answers":[["artful"]],"explanation":"A clever deceptive substitution is artful. Artless or transparent actions would not provide the calculated disguise described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":88,"targetId":88,"type":"tc","text":"The witness could {{1}} her objection with precision, but the chair dismissed its substance, confusing fluency with {{2}}.","choices":[["articulate","retract","obscure"],["verbosity","succinctness","reticence"]],"answers":[["articulate"],["verbosity"]],"explanation":"Expressing the objection clearly is to articulate it. The chair wrongly equates clear fluent speech with excessive wording, or verbosity; reticence means saying little.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"verbosity","meaning":"Use of excessive words"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":89,"targetId":89,"type":"tc","text":"The child's praise was {{1}}, offered without calculation or any hope of gaining a favor.","choices":[["artless","strategic","sycophantic","affected","disingenuous"]],"answers":[["artless"]],"explanation":"Uncalculated sincerity is artlessness. The other choices imply deliberate strategy, flattery, or pretense.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":90,"targetId":90,"type":"tc","text":"Before assigning blame, the investigators sought to {{1}} whether the records were genuine; without that step, their conclusions would be merely {{2}}.","choices":[["ascertain","obfuscate","stipulate"],["conjectural","demonstrable","incontrovertible"]],"answers":[["ascertain"],["conjectural"]],"explanation":"They must ascertain, or establish, authenticity. Without confirmation, conclusions remain conjectural rather than demonstrated or beyond dispute.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":91,"targetId":91,"type":"tc","text":"His {{1}} habits surprised wealthy visitors: he slept on a bare mat and deliberately rejected comforts he could easily afford.","choices":[["ascetic","sybaritic","ostentatious","acquisitive","indulgent"]],"answers":[["ascetic"]],"explanation":"Deliberate rejection of available comforts shows ascetic self-denial. It is not involuntary deprivation or indulgence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":92,"targetId":92,"type":"se","text":"With no evidence of sabotage, the engineers chose to {{1}} the failure to metal fatigue, identifying wear rather than malice as its cause.","choices":[["ascribe","attribute","dissociate","juxtapose","liken","subordinate"]],"answers":[["ascribe","attribute"]],"explanation":"Assigning a cause means ascribing or attributing the failure to it. Comparison and juxtaposition do not establish causation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"attribute","meaning":"Regard as caused by or belonging to"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":93,"targetId":93,"type":"tc","text":"Investors looked {{1}} at the promise of enormous profits without risk, regarding the reassuring language as a reason for suspicion.","choices":[["askance","adoringly","impartially","nostalgically","abstractly"]],"answers":[["askance"]],"explanation":"Suspicion supports looking askance. Admiration, impartiality, nostalgia, and abstraction do not convey distrust.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":94,"targetId":94,"type":"tc","text":"The candidate dismissed the charge as an {{1}}, but the newspaper argued that documentary evidence made it a {{2}} allegation.","choices":[["aspersion","accolade","axiom"],["substantiated","gratuitous","baseless"]],"answers":[["aspersion"],["substantiated"]],"explanation":"The candidate calls it a damaging smear, an aspersion. The newspaper counters that evidence makes it substantiated, not baseless or gratuitous.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"substantiated","meaning":"Supported by evidence"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":95,"targetId":95,"type":"tc","text":"The critic was quick to {{1}} the theory's assumptions but slow to supply an alternative; her attack was {{2}}, yet its lack of a constructive proposal made the overall response {{3}}.","choices":[["assail","endorse","canonize"],["trenchant","anodyne","incoherent"],["incomplete","comprehensive","definitive"]],"answers":[["assail"],["trenchant"],["incomplete"]],"explanation":"A forceful attack assails assumptions and may be trenchant. Without an alternative the response remains incomplete; sharp criticism does not automatically make it comprehensive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"incomplete","meaning":"Not containing everything needed"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":96,"targetId":96,"type":"se","text":"Training made the junior lawyer more {{1}}, able to state her position confidently without becoming aggressive toward colleagues.","choices":[["assertive","self-assured","abject","diffident","servile","evasive"]],"answers":[["assertive","self-assured"]],"explanation":"Confidence in stating a position supports assertive and self-assured. Diffidence, servility, and evasion contradict that confidence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"self-assured","meaning":"Confident in one's judgment or abilities"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":97,"targetId":97,"type":"tc","text":"The detailed explanation did little to {{1}} the families' anxiety; uncertainty about the missing passengers persisted.","choices":[["assuage","engender","corroborate","articulate","exacerbate"]],"answers":[["assuage"]],"explanation":"The expected benefit of an explanation is relief, so it did little to assuage anxiety. The remaining uncertainty shows that relief was limited.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":98,"targetId":98,"type":"tc","text":"The critic's {{1}} prose stripped away the ceremony's sentimentality; readers expecting a {{2}} tribute instead encountered severe judgments and unsparing irony.","choices":[["astringent","mellifluous","soporific"],["saccharine","caustic","dispassionate"]],"answers":[["astringent"],["saccharine"]],"explanation":"Severe unsparing irony is astringent. It contrasts with the excessively sentimental, saccharine tribute readers expected; caustic would resemble the actual criticism.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"saccharine","meaning":"Excessively sweet or sentimental"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":99,"targetId":99,"type":"tc","text":"The diplomat was {{1}} enough to notice that the apparently generous offer would bind her country to far more costly future obligations.","choices":[["astute","gullible","impetuous","incurious","naive"]],"answers":[["astute"]],"explanation":"Recognizing hidden costs requires astute judgment. Gullibility and naivety would make the apparent generosity misleading.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":100,"targetId":100,"type":"tc","text":"The barrier could {{1}} the noise but could not {{2}} it; measurements showed a substantial reduction rather than complete silence.","choices":[["attenuate","amplify","disseminate"],["eliminate","moderate","transmit"]],"answers":[["attenuate"],["eliminate"]],"explanation":"Attenuate means reduce intensity; eliminate means remove entirely. The contrast is explicitly between reduction and complete silence.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"eliminate","meaning":"Remove completely"}],"section":2,"initial":"a"},{"id":101,"targetId":101,"type":"tc","text":"The historian's proposal was {{1}}: she planned to overturn a century of scholarship using a neglected collection that nobody else had thought relevant.","choices":[["audacious","timorous","conventional","perfunctory","deferential"]],"answers":[["audacious"]],"explanation":"Challenging a century of scholarship is a daring undertaking, making audacious appropriate. Timidity and deference would resist such a challenge.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":102,"targetId":102,"type":"se","text":"The apprentice's {{1}} astonished the board: despite lacking experience, he openly challenged the founder's judgment in front of the entire company.","choices":[["audacity","temerity","reticence","humility","servility","credulity"]],"answers":[["audacity","temerity"]],"explanation":"Openly challenging a powerful founder displays audacity and temerity, both daring boldness. Humility and servility suggest the opposite behavior.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":103,"targetId":103,"type":"tc","text":"The museum hoped to {{1}} its tiny collection through loans; these would {{2}} visitors an opportunity to see works it could never afford to buy.","choices":[["augment","liquidate","curtail"],["afford","deny","obviate"]],"answers":[["augment"],["afford"]],"explanation":"Loans increase, or augment, the collection and afford an opportunity to view otherwise unaffordable works. Deny and obviate would remove that opportunity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"afford","meaning":"Provide or make available"}],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":104,"targetId":104,"type":"tc","text":"The children's informal costumes seemed incongruous in the {{1}} chamber, where solemn portraits and monumental columns conveyed generations of institutional dignity.","choices":[["august","jaunty","prosaic","ramshackle","nondescript"]],"answers":[["august"]],"explanation":"Institutional dignity and monumental surroundings make the chamber august. Informality contrasts with this impressive solemnity, not with a nondescript or ramshackle space.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":105,"targetId":105,"type":"tc","text":"The venture's first profitable month looked {{1}}, but subsequent losses created {{2}} conditions for expansion; without new funding, the larger plan became {{3}}.","choices":[["auspicious","ominous","inconsequential"],["unpropitious","providential","congenial"],["unviable","lucrative","expeditious"]],"answers":[["auspicious"],["unpropitious"],["unviable"]],"explanation":"Early profit is auspicious. Later losses make conditions unpropitious and an unfunded expansion unviable; favorable or profitable alternatives contradict that reversal.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unpropitious","meaning":"Unfavorable for success"},{"word":"unviable","meaning":"Unable to succeed or survive"}],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":106,"targetId":106,"type":"se","text":"After rejecting every decorative proposal, the designer produced an {{1}} interior whose bare surfaces carried no embellishment at all.","choices":[["austere","unadorned","ornate","flamboyant","baroque","sumptuous"]],"answers":[["austere","unadorned"]],"explanation":"Bare, undecorated surfaces make the interior austere and unadorned. The other choices imply elaborate decoration or luxury.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"unadorned","meaning":"Without decorative additions"}],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":107,"targetId":107,"type":"tc","text":"The magnate's {{1}} appetite was never satisfied: each acquisition merely intensified his desire to own more.","choices":[["avaricious","ascetic","altruistic","abstemious","philanthropic"]],"answers":[["avaricious"]],"explanation":"An insatiable desire for ownership is avaricious. Ascetic and abstemious restraint, or concern for others, does not match it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":108,"targetId":108,"type":"tc","text":"The witness continued to {{1}} that the document was authentic, but confident assertion alone did not make its provenance {{2}}.","choices":[["aver","insinuate","retract"],["unassailable","questionable","obscure"]],"answers":[["aver"],["unassailable"]],"explanation":"A confident statement is to aver. Confidence without evidence cannot establish an unassailable provenance; questionable or obscure would not name the certainty she is trying to establish.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unassailable","meaning":"Beyond successful challenge"}],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":109,"targetId":109,"type":"tc","text":"Despite his professional interest in public debate, the scholar had such an {{1}} to personal publicity that he declined all television appearances.","choices":[["aversion","affinity","inclination","obligation","entitlement"]],"answers":[["aversion"]],"explanation":"Declining publicity despite professional opportunities indicates an aversion to it. Affinity and inclination would favor participation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":110,"targetId":110,"type":"tc","text":"The assumption had once seemed {{1}}, requiring no defense; new counterexamples, however, made it {{2}} in its original unrestricted form.","choices":[["axiomatic","speculative","paradoxical"],["untenable","irrefutable","axiological"]],"answers":[["axiomatic"],["untenable"]],"explanation":"An assumption accepted without proof seems axiomatic. Counterexamples make its unrestricted version untenable, rather than irrefutable; axiological concerns values, not validity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"untenable","meaning":"Unable to be justified"}],"section":3,"initial":"a"},{"id":111,"targetId":111,"type":"tc","text":"The speech's supposedly novel insight proved {{1}}: it merely repeated the familiar injunction to work hard and persevere.","choices":[["banal","heretical","recondite","unprecedented","incendiary"]],"answers":[["banal"]],"explanation":"A familiar injunction presented as novelty is banal. Obscurity, radicalism, and unprecedented content would contradict its triteness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":112,"targetId":112,"type":"se","text":"The consultant's concluding observation was a {{1}}, so obvious and familiar that nobody could identify anything new they had learned.","choices":[["banality","platitude","paradox","enigma","heresy","revelation"]],"answers":[["banality","platitude"]],"explanation":"An obvious, familiar observation is a banality or platitude. The other nouns suggest puzzlement, dissent, or new insight.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":113,"targetId":113,"type":"tc","text":"Determined to {{1}} favoritism from the selection process, the director declared that appointments based on personal connections were {{2}} to the institution's principles.","choices":[["banish","enshrine","disseminate"],["anathema","integral","appurtenant"]],"answers":[["banish"],["anathema"]],"explanation":"To expel favoritism is to banish it. Calling such appointments anathema expresses the emphatic rejection behind the policy; integral and appurtenant imply belonging or support.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"anathema","meaning":"Something intensely rejected or detested"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":114,"targetId":114,"type":"tc","text":"Although its underlying plot was simple, the novel's style was {{1}}, full of elaborate digressions, decorative metaphors, and intricate verbal flourishes.","choices":[["baroque","spartan","laconic","pedestrian","unembellished"]],"answers":[["baroque"]],"explanation":"Elaborate ornament makes the style baroque. Spartan, laconic, and unembellished describe restraint rather than flourish.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":115,"targetId":115,"type":"tc","text":"Faced with a {{1}} of personal attacks, the spokesperson refused to answer {{2}} with abuse of her own; her {{3}} manner prevented the encounter from becoming a shouting match.","choices":[["barrage","trickle","hiatus"],["vitriol","approbation","solace"],["unflappable","irascible","frenetic"]],"answers":[["barrage"],["vitriol"],["unflappable"]],"explanation":"A concentrated onslaught is a barrage. Personal abuse is vitriol, and refusing retaliation while remaining calm demonstrates an unflappable manner.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"vitriol","meaning":"Bitterly abusive language"},{"word":"unflappable","meaning":"Calm even under pressure"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":116,"targetId":116,"type":"se","text":"Despite its impressive volume of publications, the research program was intellectually {{1}}, producing no new insights or fruitful lines of inquiry.","choices":[["barren","sterile","fecund","seminal","prolific","germinal"]],"answers":[["barren","sterile"]],"explanation":"No insights or productive inquiries makes the program barren or sterile. Prolific describes its output quantity but cannot express the stated intellectual failure.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"sterile","meaning":"Unable to produce useful results"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":117,"targetId":117,"type":"tc","text":"The judge distinguished an ordinary mistake from a {{1}} act, condemning the deliberate exploitation of people unable to defend themselves.","choices":[["base","benign","venial","inadvertent","meritorious"]],"answers":[["base"]],"explanation":"Deliberate exploitation is morally dishonorable, or base. Venial suggests pardonable misconduct, which conflicts with the force of the condemnation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":118,"targetId":118,"type":"tc","text":"The songs were so {{1}} that the school edition had to {{2}} them, replacing sexual jokes with decorous language.","choices":[["bawdy","solemn","anodyne"],["bowdlerize","authenticate","exhume"]],"answers":[["bawdy"],["bowdlerize"]],"explanation":"Sexual humor makes songs bawdy. Removing or replacing the offending material is to bowdlerize, not authenticate or retrieve it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"bowdlerize","meaning":"Remove passages judged indecent"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":119,"targetId":119,"type":"tc","text":"The witness appeared {{1}} after counsel repeatedly changed the dates in his questions; she could no longer keep the sequence of events straight.","choices":[["befuddled","omniscient","composed","lucid","assured"]],"answers":[["befuddled"]],"explanation":"Inability to track the sequence indicates confusion, or being befuddled. Lucidity and assurance would contradict that response.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":120,"targetId":120,"type":"tc","text":"The laboratory's {{1}} exterior could {{2}} the sophistication of its equipment, misleading visitors into expecting equally unimpressive facilities inside.","choices":[["unprepossessing","sumptuous","ostentatious"],["belie","advertise","corroborate"]],"answers":[["unprepossessing"],["belie"]],"explanation":"An unimpressive exterior is unprepossessing. It belies the sophisticated interior by creating a false impression; advertising or corroborating would reveal rather than conceal that sophistication.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unprepossessing","meaning":"Not impressive at first sight"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":121,"targetId":121,"type":"tc","text":"The delegate's {{1}} tone undermined his stated desire for peace: every proposed concession was met with a threat rather than a counteroffer.","choices":[["belligerent","conciliatory","imperturbable","apologetic","placid"]],"answers":[["belligerent"]],"explanation":"Repeated threats reveal belligerence despite peaceful professions. Conciliatory or apologetic tones would support peace.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":122,"targetId":122,"type":"se","text":"The trust's activities were unmistakably {{1}}, providing food, shelter, and legal assistance to people who could offer nothing in return.","choices":[["beneficent","charitable","rapacious","mercenary","predatory","punitive"]],"answers":[["beneficent","charitable"]],"explanation":"Providing aid without return is beneficent and charitable. The alternatives exploit or punish recipients rather than help them.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"charitable","meaning":"Helping people in need"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":123,"targetId":123,"type":"tc","text":"The doctor was {{1}} in intention but unusually {{2}} with her time as well, continuing to visit patients long after her paid duties ended.","choices":[["benevolent","malicious","indifferent"],["unstinting","parsimonious","grudging"]],"answers":[["benevolent"],["unstinting"]],"explanation":"Good intentions are benevolent. Abundant unpaid attention is unstinting generosity, unlike grudging or sparing service.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unstinting","meaning":"Giving generously without restriction"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":124,"targetId":124,"type":"tc","text":"Unlike the destructive chemical it replaced, the new cleaning agent was {{1}} under the specified conditions, leaving both the surface and nearby vegetation unharmed.","choices":[["benign","corrosive","caustic","volatile","virulent"]],"answers":[["benign"]],"explanation":"Leaving materials and vegetation unharmed supports benign. The contrasting terms imply danger, corrosion, or instability rather than harmlessness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":125,"targetId":125,"type":"tc","text":"The coach would {{1}} players over minor lapses, but colleagues urged him not to {{2}} them for every error; mistakes that were merely {{3}} hardly justified such bitter abuse.","choices":[["berate","extol","absolve"],["vituperate","commend","exonerate"],["venial","egregious","calamitous"]],"answers":[["berate"],["vituperate"],["venial"]],"explanation":"Scolding is to berate; bitter abusive criticism is to vituperate. Minor, forgivable errors are venial, whereas egregious or calamitous errors would weaken the contrast.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"vituperate","meaning":"Rebuke with harsh abuse"},{"word":"venial","meaning":"Minor enough to be forgiven"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":126,"targetId":126,"type":"se","text":"After losing both its archive and its funding, the institute was {{1}} of the resources needed to continue even its most modest projects.","choices":[["bereft","deprived","replete","possessed","assured","redolent"]],"answers":[["bereft","deprived"]],"explanation":"Loss of necessary resources makes it bereft or deprived of them. Replete indicates abundance; the other alternatives do not express deprivation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"deprived","meaning":"Lacking something formerly possessed or needed"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":127,"targetId":127,"type":"tc","text":"Though he claimed complete indifference to the verdict, his trembling hands seemed to {{1}} an anxiety he could not otherwise conceal.","choices":[["betray","assuage","refute","extinguish","dispel"]],"answers":[["betray"]],"explanation":"An involuntary physical sign reveals, or betrays, concealed anxiety. The alternatives reduce or contradict the emotion rather than expose it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":128,"targetId":128,"type":"tc","text":"The forgery was {{1}}, its errors visible even to an amateur; whatever {{2}} the culprit had intended was defeated by careless execution.","choices":[["blatant","imperceptible","esoteric"],["artifice","candor","rectitude"]],"answers":[["blatant"],["artifice"]],"explanation":"Obvious errors make the forgery blatant. The failed deceptive scheme is artifice, whereas candor and rectitude would not involve forgery.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"artifice","meaning":"Clever means of deception"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":129,"targetId":129,"type":"tc","text":"Her {{1}} assurance that the deadline would somehow take care of itself alarmed coworkers who understood how much work remained.","choices":[["blithe","anxious","meticulous","foreboding","scrupulous"]],"answers":[["blithe"]],"explanation":"Casual unconcern about a serious deadline is blithe. Anxiety and meticulousness would accord with the coworkers' assessment instead.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":130,"targetId":130,"type":"tc","text":"The certificate was {{1}}, despite its convincing seal; once the issuing office denied its existence, the dealer's claim to an {{2}} provenance collapsed.","choices":[["bogus","authentic","veritable"],["unimpeachable","unverifiable","dubious"]],"answers":[["bogus"],["unimpeachable"]],"explanation":"The nonexistent certificate is bogus. The dealer had claimed a provenance beyond challenge, or unimpeachable; dubious provenance would already concede the problem.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unimpeachable","meaning":"Beyond justified doubt or reproach"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":131,"targetId":131,"type":"tc","text":"The reception grew increasingly {{1}}, with exuberant guests shouting greetings across the room and drowning out the string quartet.","choices":[["boisterous","sedate","somber","reticent","funereal"]],"answers":[["boisterous"]],"explanation":"Noisy energy makes the reception boisterous. The other options imply restraint, quietness, or solemnity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":132,"targetId":132,"type":"se","text":"The newly discovered records would {{1}} the historian's argument, supplying precisely the independent evidence that its critics had demanded.","choices":[["bolster","strengthen","subvert","confound","obscure","belie"]],"answers":[["bolster","strengthen"]],"explanation":"Supporting evidence bolsters and strengthens the argument. The other verbs undermine, confuse, hide, or contradict it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"strengthen","meaning":"Make more robust or convincing"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":133,"targetId":133,"type":"tc","text":"The minister's {{1}} speeches offered inflated language in place of analysis; his much-{{2}} eloquence impressed listeners less once they asked what he had actually proposed.","choices":[["bombastic","terse","pellucid"],["vaunted","derided","concealed"]],"answers":[["bombastic"],["vaunted"]],"explanation":"Inflated rhetoric is bombastic. The later disappointment contrasts with previously vaunted eloquence; derided or concealed eloquence would not create that reversal.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"vaunted","meaning":"Widely praised or boasted about"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":134,"targetId":134,"type":"tc","text":"Interrupting the host, ridiculing the food, and refusing to thank anyone made the guest's behavior unmistakably {{1}}.","choices":[["boorish","urbane","chivalrous","deferential","punctilious"]],"answers":[["boorish"]],"explanation":"The pattern is rude and uncultured, or boorish. The alternatives emphasize politeness and consideration.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":135,"targetId":135,"type":"tc","text":"What initially looked like {{1}} proved to be genuine composure: the climber acted with {{2}} during the rescue, though afterward she remained {{3}} about the details rather than boasting.","choices":[["bravado","trepidation","servility"],["aplomb","panic","indecision"],["unforthcoming","voluble","effusive"]],"answers":[["bravado"],["aplomb"],["unforthcoming"]],"explanation":"An apparent display of boldness is bravado, but real calm is aplomb. Her subsequent refusal to elaborate is unforthcoming behavior, unlike volubility or effusiveness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"aplomb","meaning":"Poised confidence under pressure"},{"word":"unforthcoming","meaning":"Unwilling to provide information"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":136,"targetId":136,"type":"se","text":"The official's conduct was {{1}}: he accepted the prohibited gift in full view of investigators and made no attempt to conceal it.","choices":[["brazen","shameless","furtive","circumspect","abashed","penitent"]],"answers":[["brazen","shameless"]],"explanation":"Open wrongdoing without embarrassment is brazen and shameless. Furtive concealment and abashed remorse contradict the details.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"shameless","meaning":"Showing no embarrassment about wrongdoing"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":137,"targetId":137,"type":"tc","text":"The editor would {{1}} at any suggestion that her judgments were bought, reacting with visible offense even to an oblique insinuation.","choices":[["bridle","rejoice","marvel","concur","capitulate"]],"answers":[["bridle"]],"explanation":"Reacting with offended pride is to bridle. Agreement or surrender would not convey indignation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":138,"targetId":138,"type":"tc","text":"The director would {{1}} no dissent, insisting that all decisions were hers alone; employees described this concentration of power as {{2}}.","choices":[["brook","encourage","solicit"],["autocratic","egalitarian","consultative"]],"answers":[["brook"],["autocratic"]],"explanation":"To brook dissent is to tolerate it; brook no dissent means refusing it. Exclusive personal authority is autocratic, not egalitarian or consultative.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"autocratic","meaning":"Exercising unchecked personal authority"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":139,"targetId":139,"type":"tc","text":"The painter idealized a {{1}} world of quiet orchards and grazing sheep, excluding the factories that actually dominated the valley.","choices":[["bucolic","industrial","cosmopolitan","maritime","metropolitan"]],"answers":[["bucolic"]],"explanation":"Idealized peaceful country life is bucolic. The excluded factories underline the contrast with industrial and urban settings.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":140,"targetId":140,"type":"tc","text":"In practical negotiations the newcomer was {{1}}, repeatedly mishandling elementary procedures, but as an {{2}} eager to enter elite society he remained determined to cultivate influential patrons.","choices":[["bumbling","adroit","consummate"],["arriviste","anchorite","recluse"]],"answers":[["bumbling"],["arriviste"]],"explanation":"Elementary mistakes make him bumbling. Cultivating elite patrons reveals an arriviste; a recluse or anchorite would withdraw from society.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"arriviste","meaning":"An ambitious newcomer seeking social status"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":141,"targetId":141,"type":"tc","text":"Despite the project's rejection, the team remained {{1}}, confidently discussing new applications rather than dwelling on the setback.","choices":[["buoyant","despondent","forlorn","embittered","phlegmatic"]],"answers":[["buoyant"]],"explanation":"Optimistic recovery after rejection makes the team buoyant. Phlegmatic suggests unemotional calm, not the active cheerful confidence described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":142,"targetId":142,"type":"se","text":"Once publication costs fell, small journals began to {{1}}, multiplying rapidly in disciplines that had previously supported only one or two.","choices":[["burgeon","proliferate","dwindle","coalesce","atrophy","consolidate"]],"answers":[["burgeon","proliferate"]],"explanation":"Rapid multiplication is to burgeon or proliferate. Coalescing and consolidating reduce distinct entities rather than multiply them.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":143,"targetId":143,"type":"tc","text":"The agency hoped the award would {{1}} its damaged reputation; it therefore circulated only {{2}} reviews, omitting every critical response.","choices":[["burnish","tarnish","erode"],["approbatory","admonitory","derogatory"]],"answers":[["burnish"],["approbatory"]],"explanation":"Improving the agency's image is to burnish it. Selectively publishing praise means circulating approbatory reviews; warnings and derogatory reviews would defeat that effort.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"approbatory","meaning":"Expressing approval or praise"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":144,"targetId":144,"type":"tc","text":"The author added a second independent calculation to {{1}} a conclusion that readers had initially considered insufficiently supported.","choices":[["buttress","refute","qualify","obscure","retract"]],"answers":[["buttress"]],"explanation":"Independent corroboration supports, or buttresses, the conclusion. Qualification narrows it; refutation and retraction reject it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":145,"targetId":145,"type":"tc","text":"The appeals process was so {{1}} that few could navigate its rules; officials empowered to {{2}} disputes consequently exercised nearly {{3}} discretion because effective oversight was impossible.","choices":[["byzantine","transparent","expeditious"],["adjudicate","instigate","perpetuate"],["untrammeled","circumscribed","nominal"]],"answers":[["byzantine"],["adjudicate"],["untrammeled"]],"explanation":"A confusing intricate process is byzantine. Officials adjudicate disputes, and absent effective oversight their discretion becomes untrammeled rather than limited or merely nominal.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"adjudicate","meaning":"Settle a dispute by judgment"},{"word":"untrammeled","meaning":"Free from constraint"}],"section":3,"initial":"b"},{"id":146,"targetId":146,"type":"se","text":"The rehearsal was {{1}}, with instruments clashing so harshly that the conductor stopped the ensemble before the first passage ended.","choices":[["cacophonous","discordant","mellifluous","sonorous","harmonious","euphonious"]],"answers":[["cacophonous","discordant"]],"explanation":"Harshly clashing sounds are cacophonous and discordant. Sonorous concerns fullness of sound, and the other alternatives suggest pleasing harmony.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"discordant","meaning":"Harshly clashing or lacking harmony"}],"section":3,"initial":"c"},{"id":147,"targetId":147,"type":"tc","text":"Instead of a clear public discussion, the meeting became a {{1}} of competing voices, each speaker trying to drown out the others.","choices":[["cacophony","consensus","soliloquy","hiatus","cadence"]],"answers":[["cacophony"]],"explanation":"Overlapping competing voices form a cacophony. A soliloquy is a single speaker, while consensus suggests agreement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"c"},{"id":148,"targetId":148,"type":"tc","text":"The diplomat tried to {{1}} the notoriously {{2}} governor, praising his judgment in hopes of coaxing cooperation from a man who was usually irritable and hostile.","choices":[["cajole","castigate","coerce"],["bilious","genial","equable"]],"answers":[["cajole"],["bilious"]],"explanation":"Flattery used to coax is cajolery. Irritable hostility is biliousness; genial and equable would contradict his usual manner.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"bilious","meaning":"Habitually irritable or ill-tempered"}],"section":3,"initial":"c"},{"id":149,"targetId":149,"type":"tc","text":"The memoir exposes how {{1}} the young officer had been: he mistook ceremonial rank for practical competence and understood little of the work.","choices":[["callow","seasoned","astute","worldly","prescient"]],"answers":[["callow"]],"explanation":"Inexperience and immature assumptions make him callow. Seasoned and worldly imply the experience he lacks.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":3,"initial":"c"},{"id":150,"targetId":150,"type":"tc","text":"The pamphlet's accusation was {{1}}, not criticism supported by evidence; even its author privately admitted that the story was an {{2}} invention.","choices":[["calumny","panegyric","verification"],["arrant","empirical","independent"]],"answers":[["calumny"],["arrant"]],"explanation":"A knowingly false damaging accusation is calumny. Arrant intensifies its complete fabrication; empirical would falsely imply evidential grounding.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"arrant","meaning":"Utter or complete in a negative sense"}],"section":3,"initial":"c"},{"id":151,"targetId":151,"type":"tc","text":"The director's account was unusually {{1}}: she acknowledged her own mistakes as readily as she described the team's achievements.","choices":[["candid","evasive","self-serving","disingenuous","opaque"]],"answers":[["candid"]],"explanation":"Freely acknowledging her mistakes makes the account candid. Evasion and self-serving distortion would conceal or minimize those failures.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":152,"targetId":152,"type":"se","text":"The buyer proved {{1}}, recognizing hidden costs in the apparently generous contract and negotiating their removal before signing.","choices":[["canny","shrewd","credulous","improvident","naive","heedless"]],"answers":[["canny","shrewd"]],"explanation":"Recognizing and removing hidden costs demonstrates canny or shrewd judgment. The other choices imply gullibility or poor attention to consequences.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"shrewd","meaning":"Practically clever in judgment"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":153,"targetId":153,"type":"tc","text":"Later critics tried to {{1}} the poet, treating every draft as beyond reproach; this literary {{2}} obscured the unevenness that earlier readers had freely acknowledged.","choices":[["canonize","disparage","marginalize"],["apotheosis","vilification","expurgation"]],"answers":[["canonize"],["apotheosis"]],"explanation":"Treating every work as authoritative is to canonize the poet. Such exaltation is apotheosis, whereas vilification would disparage him and expurgation would remove material.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"apotheosis","meaning":"Elevation to exalted or divine status"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":154,"targetId":154,"type":"tc","text":"The union refused to {{1}} merely because negotiations had become exhausting; it would abandon its central demand only if members voted to surrender it.","choices":[["capitulate","deliberate","remonstrate","convene","persevere"]],"answers":[["capitulate"]],"explanation":"Giving up a central demand is to capitulate. Continued deliberation or perseverance would not amount to surrender.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":155,"targetId":155,"type":"tc","text":"The patron's judgments were {{1}}, reversing without explanation from one day to the next; artists therefore regarded his praise as {{2}}, and sought a more {{3}} basis for evaluating their work.","choices":[["capricious","consistent","methodical"],["evanescent","immutable","conclusive"],["stable","mercurial","erratic"]],"answers":[["capricious"],["evanescent"],["stable"]],"explanation":"Unexplained reversals are capricious. Praise likely to vanish is evanescent, prompting a search for stable standards; mercurial and erratic reproduce the original problem.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"stable","meaning":"Not subject to sudden change"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":156,"targetId":156,"type":"se","text":"The reviewer was {{1}}, ignoring the argument's substance while repeatedly finding fault with insignificant typographical details.","choices":[["captious","carping","laudatory","indulgent","imperturbable","munificent"]],"answers":[["captious","carping"]],"explanation":"Fixation on petty defects makes the review captious and carping. Praise and indulgence oppose fault-finding; calmness and generosity do not identify it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"carping","meaning":"Persistently finding trivial faults"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":157,"targetId":157,"type":"tc","text":"The report did more than note the administration's failures: it proceeded to {{1}} officials in terms so severe that several resigned.","choices":[["castigate","exculpate","placate","canonize","indemnify"]],"answers":[["castigate"]],"explanation":"Severe criticism is to castigate. Exculpation, appeasement, exaltation, and compensation would not express condemnation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":158,"targetId":158,"type":"tc","text":"The disaster was {{1}}, transforming the entire landscape, yet the historian rejected the claim that the survivors were culturally {{2}}: their sophisticated institutions endured.","choices":[["cataclysmic","trifling","imperceptible"],["benighted","enlightened","erudite"]],"answers":[["cataclysmic"],["benighted"]],"explanation":"Landscape-wide transformation is cataclysmic. Sophisticated surviving institutions rebut the claim of benighted ignorance, not enlightenment or learning.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"benighted","meaning":"Lacking knowledge or understanding"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":159,"targetId":159,"type":"tc","text":"For the witnesses, speaking openly proved {{1}}: the testimony released grief that years of enforced silence had left unresolved.","choices":[["cathartic","constricting","perfunctory","mercenary","inconsequential"]],"answers":[["cathartic"]],"explanation":"Emotional release through expression is cathartic. The alternatives fail to capture the relief explicitly described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":160,"targetId":160,"type":"tc","text":"The critic's {{1}} wit delighted readers who enjoyed cutting sarcasm, but its habitual {{2}} alienated writers who hoped for a gentler appraisal.","choices":[["caustic","anodyne","reverential"],["asperity","leniency","diffidence"]],"answers":[["caustic"],["asperity"]],"explanation":"Cutting sarcasm is caustic. Harshness, or asperity, contrasts with the gentle treatment writers wanted; leniency would satisfy that hope.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"asperity","meaning":"Harshness of tone or manner"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":161,"targetId":161,"type":"tc","text":"The engineer's {{1}} attitude toward repeated safety warnings ended only when a near accident made dismissing them impossible.","choices":[["cavalier","meticulous","vigilant","solicitous","circumspect"]],"answers":[["cavalier"]],"explanation":"Casually dismissing serious warnings is cavalier. Vigilant and circumspect behavior would take them seriously from the start.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":162,"targetId":162,"type":"se","text":"Once both sides signed the truce, hostilities were supposed to {{1}}, bringing the prolonged fighting to an end.","choices":[["cease","stop","escalate","recur","proliferate","persist"]],"answers":[["cease","stop"]],"explanation":"Ending the fighting requires cease and stop. The other verbs describe continuation, recurrence, or increase.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"stop","meaning":"Come to an end"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":163,"targetId":163,"type":"tc","text":"The committee voted to {{1}} the director formally, but its resolution imposed no financial penalty; the sanction was one of official {{2}}.","choices":[["censure","absolve","indemnify"],["disapprobation","restitution","exoneration"]],"answers":[["censure"],["disapprobation"]],"explanation":"Formal condemnation is censure, expressed through disapprobation. Restitution would involve repayment, and exoneration would clear the director.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"disapprobation","meaning":"Strong disapproval"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":164,"targetId":164,"type":"tc","text":"The play's appeal was primarily {{1}}, inviting audiences to analyze elaborate arguments rather than identify emotionally with its characters.","choices":[["cerebral","visceral","sensual","maudlin","instinctive"]],"answers":[["cerebral"]],"explanation":"Analysis and intellectual argument make the appeal cerebral. Visceral and instinctive reactions contrast with reflective thought.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":165,"targetId":165,"type":"tc","text":"To her {{1}}, the mathematician discovered that the result she had announced as novel was well known; the discovery {{2}} her earlier claim but did not {{3}} the accuracy of her proof.","choices":[["chagrin","exultation","complacency"],["invalidated","corroborated","authenticated"],["impugn","establish","demonstrate"]],"answers":[["chagrin"],["invalidated"],["impugn"]],"explanation":"Embarrassment is chagrin. Prior knowledge invalidates the novelty claim, but it does not impugn, or cast doubt on, the proof's correctness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"invalidated","meaning":"Made a claim no longer defensible"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":166,"targetId":166,"type":"se","text":"After losing money on an earlier venture, the board was {{1}} of bold forecasts, examining every assumption before approving another investment.","choices":[["chary","wary","sanguine","credulous","heedless","ebullient"]],"answers":[["chary","wary"]],"explanation":"Caution after a loss makes the board chary and wary. Optimism, gullibility, and heedlessness contradict its careful scrutiny.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":167,"targetId":167,"type":"tc","text":"The mentor chose to {{1}} the apprentice for concealing the error, stressing that an honest mistake was less serious than deliberate deception.","choices":[["chastise","commend","absolve","flatter","emulate"]],"answers":[["chastise"]],"explanation":"Concealment earns a rebuke, so chastise fits. Praise and absolution would deny the mentor's stated judgment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":168,"targetId":168,"type":"tc","text":"The editorial was {{1}}, assuming that national origin alone guaranteed superiority; historians later described this unquestioning loyalty as a {{2}} influence on public debate.","choices":[["chauvinistic","cosmopolitan","impartial"],["baleful","salutary","ameliorative"]],"answers":[["chauvinistic"],["baleful"]],"explanation":"Blind national superiority is chauvinistic. The criticism of this bias makes its influence baleful, not beneficial or improving.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"baleful","meaning":"Threatening harm or evil"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":169,"targetId":169,"type":"tc","text":"The archivist continued to {{1}} the worn notebook, not for its market price but because its pages preserved her grandmother's voice.","choices":[["cherish","repudiate","disparage","liquidate","eschew"]],"answers":[["cherish"]],"explanation":"Deep personal value leads her to cherish the notebook. Its market price is explicitly distinguished from the emotional reason for keeping it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":170,"targetId":170,"type":"tc","text":"The negotiation involved considerable {{1}}: one party concealed essential conditions in misleading footnotes, making the apparent agreement an exercise in {{2}}.","choices":[["chicanery","rectitude","magnanimity"],["artifice","reciprocity","candor"]],"answers":[["chicanery"],["artifice"]],"explanation":"Misleading concealed conditions are chicanery and artifice. Honest mutual agreement would involve candor and reciprocity instead.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"artifice","meaning":"Clever means of deception"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":171,"targetId":171,"type":"tc","text":"The officer's {{1}} behavior extended even to defeated opponents, whom he protected from humiliation and treated with unfailing courtesy.","choices":[["chivalrous","predatory","vindictive","boorish","mercenary"]],"answers":[["chivalrous"]],"explanation":"Protective honor and courtesy, even toward opponents, are chivalrous. Revenge, exploitation, and rudeness contradict the conduct.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":172,"targetId":172,"type":"se","text":"Hearing the absurdly happy ending, the normally solemn judge began to {{1}}, laughing softly with evident pleasure.","choices":[["chortle","chuckle","lament","fulminate","remonstrate","brood"]],"answers":[["chortle","chuckle"]],"explanation":"Pleasurable laughter supports chortle and chuckle. Lamenting, raging, protesting, and brooding do not express amusement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"chuckle","meaning":"Laugh softly with amusement"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":173,"targetId":173,"type":"tc","text":"His {{1}} reply to the invitation offended even close friends; what could have been a polite refusal became an act of needless {{2}}.","choices":[["churlish","gracious","deferential"],["incivility","solicitude","beneficence"]],"answers":[["churlish"],["incivility"]],"explanation":"An ill-tempered offensive reply is churlish, and needless rudeness is incivility. Concern and kindness would not account for the offense.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"incivility","meaning":"Rudeness or discourtesy"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":174,"targetId":174,"type":"tc","text":"The charter was designed to {{1}} executive power by specifying actions the officeholder could never authorize alone.","choices":[["circumscribe","aggrandize","sanctify","perpetuate","usurp"]],"answers":[["circumscribe"]],"explanation":"Specifying limits circumscribes power. Aggrandizing enlarges it, and perpetuating maintains its duration rather than restricting its scope.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":175,"targetId":175,"type":"tc","text":"A {{1}} editor would verify the anonymous allegation before publication; such {{2}} is essential when reputational harm may be {{3}}, persisting even after a retraction.","choices":[["circumspect","impetuous","credulous"],["prudence","temerity","credulity"],["irreversible","ephemeral","negligible"]],"answers":[["circumspect"],["prudence"],["irreversible"]],"explanation":"Careful verification is circumspect prudence. Damage surviving a retraction may be irreversible; rashness and gullibility would increase this danger.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"prudence","meaning":"Careful judgment that avoids needless risk"},{"word":"irreversible","meaning":"Unable to be undone"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":176,"targetId":176,"type":"se","text":"The company sought to {{1}} the restriction by routing transactions through a subsidiary, hoping to get around the rule without openly challenging it.","choices":[["circumvent","bypass","enforce","ratify","extend","publicize"]],"answers":[["circumvent","bypass"]],"explanation":"Routing around a rule circumvents or bypasses it. Enforcement, ratification, extension, and publicity do not evade its effect.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"bypass","meaning":"Find a way around a restriction"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":177,"targetId":177,"type":"tc","text":"The crowd became {{1}} when the doors remained closed, repeatedly shouting demands that the officials inside could no longer ignore.","choices":[["clamorous","quiescent","reticent","diffident","compliant"]],"answers":[["clamorous"]],"explanation":"Loud repeated demands make the crowd clamorous. The other choices imply quietness, timidity, or acceptance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":178,"targetId":178,"type":"tc","text":"The correspondence was {{1}}, exchanged through intermediaries to avoid detection; its later discovery exposed an agreement the participants had tried to keep {{2}}.","choices":[["clandestine","public","ostentatious"],["covert","notorious","manifest"]],"answers":[["clandestine"],["covert"]],"explanation":"Avoiding detection makes the correspondence clandestine and the agreement covert. The alternative answers imply visibility or public knowledge.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":179,"targetId":179,"type":"tc","text":"The sudden {{1}} of falling metal trays drowned out the lecturer, whose words disappeared beneath their harsh ringing.","choices":[["clangor","murmur","susurration","silence","whisper"]],"answers":[["clangor"]],"explanation":"Loud metallic clashing is clangor. The other sound terms are quiet, and silence is the absence of sound.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":180,"targetId":180,"type":"tc","text":"The report tried to {{1}} its author's commercial interest in the language of public service, but the undisclosed payments made that posture {{2}}.","choices":[["cloak","reveal","advertise"],["disingenuous","candid","disinterested"]],"answers":[["cloak"],["disingenuous"]],"explanation":"Concealing private interest is to cloak it. Secret payments undermine the professed public motive, making the posture disingenuous rather than candid or impartial.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":181,"targetId":181,"type":"tc","text":"Separate local campaigns began to {{1}} into a national movement once their organizers recognized a common objective.","choices":[["coalesce","fragment","dissipate","diverge","stagnate"]],"answers":[["coalesce"]],"explanation":"Coming together into one movement is coalescing. Fragmentation and divergence would keep the campaigns apart.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":182,"targetId":182,"type":"se","text":"The signatures were obtained through {{1}}, as employees were threatened with dismissal if they refused to sign.","choices":[["coercion","compulsion","persuasion","consensus","solicitude","largesse"]],"answers":[["coercion","compulsion"]],"explanation":"A threat to employment creates coercion or compulsion. The alternatives do not identify forced agreement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"compulsion","meaning":"Pressure that forces someone to act"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":183,"targetId":183,"type":"tc","text":"The argument was {{1}}, connecting each conclusion to explicit evidence; opponents therefore had to challenge its premises rather than dismiss it as {{2}}.","choices":[["cogent","incoherent","nebulous"],["specious","demonstrable","rigorous"]],"answers":[["cogent"],["specious"]],"explanation":"Clear evidential reasoning is cogent. A merely plausible but unsound argument would be specious; demonstrable and rigorous would reinforce rather than dismiss it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":184,"targetId":184,"type":"tc","text":"Asked for an immediate answer, the philosopher requested time to {{1}}, believing that a question with such implications deserved sustained thought.","choices":[["cogitate","extemporize","capitulate","dissemble","acquiesce"]],"answers":[["cogitate"]],"explanation":"Sustained deep thought is cogitation. Extemporizing would supply an unrehearsed response, contrary to the request for more time.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":185,"targetId":185,"type":"tc","text":"The sociologist sought to {{1}} a term for the decade's prevailing mood; a successful label would capture its {{2}} without reducing a complex period to a {{3}} slogan.","choices":[["coin","expunge","decipher"],["zeitgeist","anachronism","hiatus"],["reductive","nuanced","comprehensive"]],"answers":[["coin"],["zeitgeist"],["reductive"]],"explanation":"Inventing a term is to coin it. The era's characteristic mood is its zeitgeist, and the warning concerns reductive simplification rather than nuanced representation.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"zeitgeist","meaning":"Characteristic spirit of an era"},{"word":"reductive","meaning":"Oversimplifying something complex"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":186,"targetId":186,"type":"se","text":"Rather than publish competing accounts, the two laboratories agreed to {{1}}, pooling their data and sharing responsibility for a joint study.","choices":[["collaborate","cooperate","defect","disengage","prevaricate","compete"]],"answers":[["collaborate","cooperate"]],"explanation":"Pooling data for joint work means collaborating and cooperating. Competing and disengaging contradict the arrangement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"cooperate","meaning":"Work jointly toward an objective"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":187,"targetId":187,"type":"tc","text":"The firms claimed to compete vigorously, but secret messages showed that they had agreed to {{1}} in fixing prices above competitive levels.","choices":[["collude","dissent","capitulate","abstain","exonerate"]],"answers":[["collude"]],"explanation":"Secret dishonest coordination is collusion. Dissent would oppose the arrangement, and the other verbs do not express covert cooperation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":188,"targetId":188,"type":"tc","text":"Despite profound disagreement, the judges maintained {{1}}, addressing one another with courtesy; their dissent was therefore {{2}} in substance without becoming personally abusive.","choices":[["comity","acrimony","antipathy"],["uncompromising","sycophantic","conciliatory"]],"answers":[["comity"],["uncompromising"]],"explanation":"Courtesy amid disagreement is comity. Firm, uncompromising dissent can remain polite; sycophancy would surrender independence, and conciliatory substance would soften the disagreement.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"uncompromising","meaning":"Refusing to soften a position or principle"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":189,"targetId":189,"type":"tc","text":"Work could not {{1}} until the archaeological survey was complete, because construction might destroy evidence still waiting to be examined.","choices":[["commence","cease","abate","dwindle","recur"]],"answers":[["commence"]],"explanation":"The survey must precede the beginning, or commencement, of work. Ceasing would concern work already underway.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":190,"targetId":190,"type":"tc","text":"The penalty was not {{1}} with the minor violation: its severity was so disproportionate that even supporters of strict enforcement called it {{2}}.","choices":[["commensurate","incompatible","coincident"],["draconian","lenient","perfunctory"]],"answers":[["commensurate"],["draconian"]],"explanation":"A proportionate penalty is commensurate. Excessive severity is draconian, not lenient or merely routine.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"draconian","meaning":"MISSING"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":191,"targetId":191,"type":"tc","text":"Though unable to reverse the decision, colleagues gathered to {{1}} with the rejected applicant, offering sympathy rather than advice.","choices":[["commiserate","remonstrate","exult","collude","contend"]],"answers":[["commiserate"]],"explanation":"Expressing sympathy for someone's disappointment is to commiserate. Exultation would celebrate, while remonstration protests.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":192,"targetId":192,"type":"se","text":"The documentary's evidence was {{1}}, persuading even skeptical viewers because several independent records supported the same conclusion.","choices":[["compelling","convincing","fallacious","tenuous","equivocal","specious"]],"answers":[["compelling","convincing"]],"explanation":"Convergent independent evidence makes the case compelling and convincing. The alternatives describe weak, ambiguous, or misleading support.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"convincing","meaning":"Able to persuade through strong reasons"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":193,"targetId":193,"type":"tc","text":"The department needed a {{1}} administrator, not necessarily a visionary; dependable execution of routine duties mattered more than {{2}} innovations.","choices":[["competent","inept","negligent"],["radical","incremental","modest"]],"answers":[["competent"],["radical"]],"explanation":"Sufficient dependable ability is competence. The contrast with visionary work points to radical innovations, rather than merely modest changes.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":194,"targetId":194,"type":"tc","text":"Early success made the team {{1}}: convinced its lead was secure, it stopped monitoring competitors and missed a major shift in demand.","choices":[["complacent","vigilant","restive","apprehensive","circumspect"]],"answers":[["complacent"]],"explanation":"Self-satisfaction that overlooks risk is complacency. Vigilance and circumspection would sustain attention to competitors.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":195,"targetId":195,"type":"tc","text":"The collaborators' skills were {{1}}: one generated bold hypotheses while the other tested them with {{2}} care, so together they produced work neither could {{3}} alone.","choices":[["complementary","identical","incompatible"],["meticulous","cursory","desultory"],["accomplish","disparage","renounce"]],"answers":[["complementary"],["meticulous"],["accomplish"]],"explanation":"Different mutually useful strengths are complementary. Careful testing is meticulous, and collaboration enables work neither could accomplish independently.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"accomplish","meaning":"Bring successfully to completion"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":196,"targetId":196,"type":"se","text":"Although they disputed the regulation, the researchers agreed to {{1}} with its requirements while seeking a legal amendment.","choices":[["comply","conform","dispense","tamper","quarrel","interfere"]],"answers":[["comply","conform"]],"explanation":"Following requirements despite disagreement means complying or conforming. Dispensing or tampering would evade or alter them rather than obey them.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"conform","meaning":"Act in accordance with a rule"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":197,"targetId":197,"type":"tc","text":"Allowing donors to select research outcomes would {{1}} the laboratory's independence, even if the funding solved its immediate financial problems.","choices":[["compromise","safeguard","substantiate","exemplify","ratify"]],"answers":[["compromise"]],"explanation":"External control over outcomes would weaken or endanger independence: compromise it. Safeguarding would do the opposite.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":198,"targetId":198,"type":"tc","text":"The minister would {{1}} that the figures were wrong, but refused to accept that the error entitled his opponents to {{2}} powers assigned to an independent commission.","choices":[["concede","dispute","conceal"],["arrogate","relinquish","abrogate"]],"answers":[["concede"],["arrogate"]],"explanation":"Admitting the error is to concede it. Taking powers assigned elsewhere is to arrogate them; relinquishing gives them up, while abrogating revokes rather than seizes them.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"arrogate","meaning":"Claim authority without a right"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":199,"targetId":199,"type":"tc","text":"The letter's {{1}} tone surprised readers expecting retaliation: it acknowledged shared concerns and offered a meeting to repair relations.","choices":[["conciliatory","bellicose","caustic","derisive","accusatory"]],"answers":[["conciliatory"]],"explanation":"Shared concerns and a meeting aimed at repair make the tone conciliatory. The alternatives intensify hostility rather than reduce it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":4,"initial":"c"},{"id":200,"targetId":200,"type":"tc","text":"The experiment was {{1}} on the narrow question it tested, but its results could not justify the author's much broader {{2}} about every related phenomenon.","choices":[["conclusive","inconclusive","ambiguous"],["extrapolation","retraction","qualification"]],"answers":[["conclusive"],["extrapolation"]],"explanation":"Decisively settling the tested question is conclusive. Extending that result to all related phenomena is extrapolation, whereas retraction and qualification would narrow claims.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"extrapolation","meaning":"MISSING"}],"section":4,"initial":"c"}]);
